course: dictionary
lesson: 3304
title: Ethiopian: a word about a place and its people
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ethiopian can be an adjective or a noun. As an adjective it describes things from Ethiopia, a country in East Africa, such as Ethiopian food or Ethiopian music. As a noun it means a person from Ethiopia. It always begins with a capital letter because it names a place.
explain: Look at the context. «The Ethiopian calendar has 12 months of 30 days and a short 13th month of 5 or 6 days.» Here Ethiopian describes the calendar, so it means the calendar used in Ethiopia. The word is describing a thing, so it is an adjective.
explain: Try the swap test. Put "from Ethiopia" in place of Ethiopian: "the calendar from Ethiopia has 12 months" still makes sense, so your meaning is right. Ask yourself: is the word describing something, or is it naming a person? That tells you which meaning to use.
fact: Ethiopian means from or to do with Ethiopia, a country in East Africa.
fact: As a noun, an Ethiopian is a person from Ethiopia. The plural is Ethiopians.
fact: Ethiopia is in the Horn of Africa, and its capital is Addis Ababa.
fact: Ethiopia has high mountains and an ancient history.
fact: Ethiopian food includes injera, a spongy flatbread, and many Ethiopian runners have won Olympic medals.
exercise: «Ethiopian food includes injera, a spongy flatbread.» What does Ethiopian mean in this sentence? | A person who is very hungry | *From or to do with Ethiopia | A type of flat river boat | A large mountain
exercise: What is the capital city of Ethiopia? | *Addis Ababa | Cairo | Paris | Nairobi
exercise: Ethiopia is in the Horn of Africa. Which continent is it in? | Europe | Asia | *Africa | South America
exercise: Which sentence uses Ethiopian as a noun, meaning a person? | The Ethiopian Orthodox Church is very old | Injera is a spongy Ethiopian bread | *Her friend is an Ethiopian who moved to our town. | The Ethiopian calendar has 13 months
test: In the Ethiopian calendar, which month does the New Year come in? | January | March | *September | December
test: Why does the Ethiopian calendar number its years differently from ours? | It has only seven months | Its years are much longer than ours | It starts its calendar in the middle of summer | *It counts years differently, so its year number is about 7 or 8 behind ours
exam: «New Year can be in winter, spring or autumn: Nowruz, the Persian New Year, comes on the first day of spring.» Which word in this sentence names a different New Year from the Ethiopian one? | *Nowruz | autumn | spring | winter
